tests:
  unit:
    count: 26
    covers: "api.test.ts (+4): a refused listing throws ApiError carrying 400 while a dead socket throws no ApiError at all - the discrimination asserted as the presence and absence of a type rather than a flag; every non-ok answer keeps its own status over 400/404/500/503; apiAddr follows the configured base URL without its scheme, so the words on screen track DEC-12's configuration rather than a literal; and the stream reports its transport going down and coming back without those being read as flow events. useFlows.test.ts (+14): the two facts held apart - silence and a refusal, each with one owner; rows survive silence (discarding evidence on a transient failure is the worse error) and go with a refusal (the old list is not an answer to the query that was refused); a refusal names the value that was ASKED, verified red against the old line that read the box at response time; a dropped stream records silence without unsaying a refusal, and silence ends when a listing arrives rather than when the stream reconnects; a straggling refresh cannot overwrite the answer a later one gave; retrying is true for the whole fetch (fork D4's half the hook owns); useFlowDetail reports any fetch that produced no flow, and a bumped nonce re-runs it. FlowList.test.tsx (+8): the four absences and the precedence the RUN states (a silent core outranks both empties, asserted for filtered and unfiltered) - plus the two the build refused to invent: a refusal is not hidden by the silence that followed it, and the trailing clause is said only when it is true of the region below. Notice.test.tsx and Button.test.tsx are deliberately UNEDITED: every reuse is per contract, and a component test changing would mean the design chose wrong."
  integration:
    count: 16
    covers: "ui/src/App.test.tsx, driven through a scripted TrawlerApi rather than a mocked fetch, because the bounded contract is the shell as a whole reacting to what the API does. The status field's three changes; a class reaching the wire as typed; a core that stops answering mid-session raising the band above rows that do not move, with the count unchanged; nothing captured putting the same sentence in the row region with no band, asserted as exactly one occurrence; a refused query producing the row-region rejection with the band away, the field keeping its text; retry disabling its button AND naming what is awaited in one act, then removing the band and placing focus on the list region; a retry that fails again keeping the band and leaving focus alone; a connection that recovers on its own NOT moving focus; the onboarding phase rendering no band. Plus six regressions from the review rounds, each red before its fix: a pane failure does not speak for the list; a pane failure is stated even when the list is empty for its own reason; a core going silent does not put the pane back on 'loading this flow...'; a pane failure states itself without repeating the connection's alarm (asserted as exactly one data-tone=warn on the page); and a core that goes silent after refusing a query states both, hiding neither."
  e2e:
    count: 2
    covers: "ui/e2e/specs/flows.spec.ts against the real WebKit window and an ephemeral core - the two assertions the epic plan assigns to W6. (1) The status class: with a 200, a 404 and a 500 captured, ?status=4xx narrows to the 4xx statuses and shows no 5xx (so 'not 2xx' cannot pass for 'is 4xx'), an exact 404 still narrows to 404, and 4xz renders the stated rejection rather than an empty list or a connection failure. (2) The unreachable statement, as a PAIR, because the epic asks for the distinguishing assertion and not just the positive one: first the control - a reachable core with a filter matching nothing renders 'no flows match this filter' and explicitly not the connection statement - then the core is killed and the band appears above rows that are still there, count unchanged, with a working retry. No user act follows the kill: the dropped SSE stream is what the shell notices, which is what W4's Interaction table requires. Harness changes were needed for both: 4xx/5xx origin routes (it produced only 2xx), and an abrupt /kill, because /stop is a graceful shutdown that waits on in-flight requests and an SSE stream never finishes - it would have held open the very connection the assertion is about. e2e: 32 passing, 0 failing, re-run green after every review round. onboarding.spec.ts is UNEDITED and green, which is this epic's standing check that the re-design really left."
suite: "pnpm test 831/831 (789 at the base, 42 added) · pnpm lint clean · tsc --noEmit clean · cargo xtask ui e2e 32/32 · cargo clippy pedantic + cargo fmt clean"
rounds: "Six review rounds. Rounds 1-5 found 13 verified defects; round 6 returned clean by case analysis rather than by the suite passing. Rounds 2, 3 and 4 each found defects INSIDE the previous round's fix, which was itself the finding: three facts (the core is not answering, the core refused this query, this flow's fetch produced nothing) were encoded in two values with precedence baked into the encoding. Round 4 split them; rounds 5 and 6 judged the split. Every verified defect got a named regression test RED FIRST before its fix, per docs/testing-strategy.html; the refusal-value regression is additionally verified red against the exact line it replaced."
